:1940-08-31-douglas-dc-3-id-4880
        rdf:type                 schema:Event;
        ex:aircraftType          "Douglas DC-3";
        ex:groundFatalities      "0"^^xsd:int;
        ex:hour                  "14:41";
        ex:operator              "Pennsylvania Central Airlines";
        ex:passengersAboard      "25"^^xsd:int;
        ex:passengersFatalities  "25"^^xsd:int;
        ex:region                "Virginia";
        ex:route                 "Washington D.C. - Detroit";
        dcterms:date             "1940-08-31"^^xsd:date;
        dcterms:description      "The aircraft was flying through a thunderstorm in turbulence when the it nosed over and plunged to earth near Short Hill Mountain.  Disabling of the pilots by a severe lightning discharge in the immediate neighborhood of the airplane, with resulting loss of control. With limited accident investigation tools at the time, the most likely cause was the plane flying into windshear.  U.S. Senator Ernest Lundeen from Minnesota killed.";
        schema:location          "Near Lovettsville" .

:1970-07-05-mcdonnell-douglas-dc-8-63-id-1122
        rdf:type                 schema:Event;
        ex:aircraftType          "McDonnell Douglas DC-8-63";
        ex:groundFatalities      "0"^^xsd:int;
        ex:hour                  "08:09";
        ex:operator              "Air Canada";
        ex:passengersAboard      "109"^^xsd:int;
        ex:passengersFatalities  "109"^^xsd:int;
        ex:region                "Canada";
        ex:route                 "Montreal - Toronto - Los Angeles";
        dcterms:date             "1970-07-05"^^xsd:date;
        dcterms:description      "While attempting to land at Toronto, at approximately 60 feet above the runway, the spoilers were inadvertently deployed causing the aircraft to fall to the runway and lose the No. 4 engine. With the wing on fire, the captain decided to go-around but while circling the plane lost the No. 3 engine and eventually went into a seep dive and crashed. Inadvertent deployment of the spoilers by the first officer while the aircraft was still in the air. Faulty design by allowing the spoiler handle to perform two different unrelated tasks (lift to arm and pull to deploy). After the crash McDonnell Douglas denied there was any design flaw and the FAA decided to issue an Airworthiness Directive requiring a warning placard on all DC8s. After two and possibly three more crashed with the same cause an AD was issued requiring a safety lock be installed.";
        schema:location          "Toronto" .

