:1976-06-04-lockheed-188a-electra-id-2132
        rdf:type                 schema:Event;
        ex:aircraftType          "Lockheed 188A Electra";
        ex:groundFatalities      "1"^^xsd:int;
        ex:hour                  "14:48";
        ex:operator              "Air Manila";
        ex:passengersAboard      "45"^^xsd:int;
        ex:passengersFatalities  "45"^^xsd:int;
        ex:region                "Guam";
        ex:route                 "Wake Island - Guam - Manila";
        dcterms:date             "1976-06-04"^^xsd:date;
        dcterms:description      "The No. 3 engine failed during the takeoff roll as the plane left Agana Naval Air Station. The aircraft began to rise but struck terrain and exploded in flames. The loss of climb capability after the crew retracted the flaps at too low an altitude to clear the rising terrain. The flaps were retracted after the No. 3 propeller feathered as the aircraft lifted off the runway. A driver in an automobile struck by the aircraft was killed.";
        schema:location          "Agana" .

:2003-07-13-cessna-402c-id-525
        rdf:type                 schema:Event;
        ex:aircraftType          "Cessna 402C";
        ex:groundFatalities      "0"^^xsd:int;
        ex:hour                  "15:30";
        ex:operator              "Air Sunshine";
        ex:passengersAboard      "10"^^xsd:int;
        ex:passengersFatalities  "2"^^xsd:int;
        ex:region                "Bahamas";
        ex:route                 "Ft. Lauderdale, FL - Treasure Cay";
        dcterms:date             "2003-07-13"^^xsd:date;
        dcterms:description      "Twenty miles west of its destination, at an altitude of 3,500 ft., the right engine began coming apart and throwing parts out of the cowling. Although the right engine was feathered, flaps and gear retracted and full power applied t the left engine, the plane could not maintain altitude and the pilot ditched the aircraft 15 miles from its destination. All passengers and the pilot were able to exit from the plane before it sank but 2 passengers subsequently died. The in-flight failure of the right engine and the pilot's failure to adequately manage the airplane's performance after the engine failed. The right engine failure resulted for inadequate maintenance that was performed by Air Sunshine's maintenance personnel during undocumented maintenance. Contributing to the passenger fatalities was the pilot's failure to provide an emergency briefing after the right engine failed.";
        schema:location          "Off Treasure Cay" .

