:1947-10-26-douglas-dc-4-1009-id-2115
        rdf:type                 schema:Event;
        ex:aircraftType          "Douglas DC-4-1009";
        ex:groundFatalities      "0"^^xsd:int;
        ex:hour                  "17:20";
        ex:operator              "AB Aerotransport";
        ex:passengersAboard      "44"^^xsd:int;
        ex:passengersFatalities  "44"^^xsd:int;
        ex:region                "Greece";
        ex:route                 "Istanbul-Athens-Rome-Geneve-Copenhagen-Stockholm";
        dcterms:date             "1947-10-26"^^xsd:date;
        dcterms:description      "The aircraft crashed into Mt. Hymettus while on approach to Hassani Airport in a severe rainstorm. The captain, John Douglas, probably tried to force the landing due to the weather conditions, when the belly of the aircraft hit the summit, resulting in loss of control, crash and fire. The pilot did not properly follow IFR procedures. The wreckage was found the next morning with the corpses of the victims plundered (the victim's pockets were found to be turned inside-out). The aircraft was named 'Sunnan.'";
        schema:location          "Near Mt. Hymettus" .

:1960-07-14-douglas-dc-7c-id-3885
        rdf:type                 schema:Event;
        ex:aircraftType          "Douglas DC-7C";
        ex:groundFatalities      "0"^^xsd:int;
        ex:hour                  "04:30";
        ex:operator              "Northwest Orient Airlines";
        ex:passengersAboard      "58"^^xsd:int;
        ex:passengersFatalities  "1"^^xsd:int;
        ex:region                "Philippines";
        ex:route                 "Okinawa - Manila";
        dcterms:date             "1960-07-14"^^xsd:date;
        dcterms:description      "After a loss of power in the No. 2 engine the crew was unable to feather the prop. An emergency descent was made and the plane ditched into the ocean. Upon contact with the water, the aft end of the fuselage broke free at the rear of the pressure bulkhead, the right wing  tore free and the engines separated. The wing floated for 3hrs, serving as a life raft for  passengers. The remainder of the fuselage, sank some 10 minutes after impact.  Passengers were rescued 6 hours after the accident. Failure of No. 2  engine, resulting in oil contamination, loss of oil supply, subsequent loss of the No. 2 propeller assembly and fire-in-flight, which necessitated a ditching.";
        schema:location          "Off Manila" .

