:1960-10-29-curtiss-c-46f-1-cu-id-3628
        rdf:type                 schema:Event;
        ex:aircraftType          "Curtiss C-46F-1-CU";
        ex:groundFatalities      "0"^^xsd:int;
        ex:hour                  "21:02";
        ex:operator              "ArctiPacific";
        ex:passengersAboard      "48"^^xsd:int;
        ex:passengersFatalities  "22"^^xsd:int;
        ex:region                "Ohio";
        ex:route                 "Toledo - Kansas City, MO";
        dcterms:date             "1960-10-29"^^xsd:date;
        dcterms:description      "Crashed after taking off from Toledo Express Airfield, landing 5,800 ft. beyond the runway threshold. Sixteen members of the California State  PolytechniCollege,  San Luis Obispo football team killed. Premature lift-off, partial loss of power in the No.1 engine, overweight aircraft and weather conditions were the cause of the crash.";
        schema:location          "Toledo" .

:2004-10-24-learjet-35a-id-935
        rdf:type                 schema:Event;
        ex:aircraftType          "Learjet 35A";
        ex:groundFatalities      "0"^^xsd:int;
        ex:hour                  "00:30";
        ex:operator              "Med Flight Air Ambulance - Private";
        ex:passengersAboard      "5"^^xsd:int;
        ex:passengersFatalities  "5"^^xsd:int;
        ex:region                "California";
        ex:route                 "San Diego, CA - Albuquerque, NM";
        dcterms:date             "2004-10-24"^^xsd:date;
        dcterms:description      "Two minutes after taking off, the air ambulance crashed into Otay peak,  in an isolated area of mountains, 8 miles east of Brown Field Municipal Airport. Dark night visual conditions prevailed. The controller's Minimum Safe Altitude Warning System issued and alert during the last two radar returns. The air trafficontroller failed to alert the crew to altitude warnings as the plane was on track to fly into a mountain. The controller identified the airplane on the radar screen and instructed crew members to expect clearance above 5,000 feet, but then issued a heading that resulted in a flight track directly into the mountains. The mountain is at 3,556 feet altitude. Also, the flight crew did not follow the recommended departure procedures for taking off at night and in mountainous terrain. Failure of the flightcrew to maintain terrain clearance during a VFR departure, which resulted in controlled flight into terrain, and the air trafficontroller's issuance of a clearance that transferred the responsibility for terrain clearance from the fllightcrew to the controller. The controller's failure to provide terrain clearance instructions to the flightcrew, and failure to advise the flightcrew of minimum safe altitude warning alerts. Contributing to the accident was the pilots' fatigue which likely contributed to their degraded decision making.";
        schema:location          "San Diego" .

:1994-08-21-aerospatiale-atr-42-300-id-3160
        rdf:type                 schema:Event;
        ex:aircraftType          "Aerospatiale ATR-42-300";
        ex:groundFatalities      "0"^^xsd:int;
        ex:operator              "Royal Air Maroc";
        ex:passengersAboard      "44"^^xsd:int;
        ex:passengersFatalities  "44"^^xsd:int;
        ex:region                "Morocco";
        ex:route                 "Agadir - Casablanca";
        dcterms:date             "1994-08-21"^^xsd:date;
        dcterms:description      "The aircraft entered a steep dive from 16,000 ft. and crashed 10 minutes after taking off from Agadir. The pilot was blamed for disconnecting the autopilot and deliberately causing the crash and committing suicide. This was challenged by the Moroccan Pilot's Union.";
        schema:location          "Near Agadir" .

:1974-12-01-boeing-b-727-251-id-3367
        rdf:type                 schema:Event;
        ex:aircraftType          "Boeing B-727-251";
        ex:groundFatalities      "0"^^xsd:int;
        ex:hour                  "19:26";
        ex:operator              "Northwest Orient Airlines";
        ex:passengersAboard      "3"^^xsd:int;
        ex:passengersFatalities  "3"^^xsd:int;
        ex:region                "New York";
        ex:route                 "New York City, NY - Buffalo, NY";
        dcterms:date             "1974-12-01"^^xsd:date;
        dcterms:description      "The aircraft stalled in a climb from which recovery was not effected. The loss of control of the aircraft because the flight crew failed to recognize and correct the aircraft's high-angle-of-attack, low-speed stall and its descending spiral. The stall was precipitated by the flight crew's improper reaction to erroneous airspeed and Mach indications which had resulted from a blockage of the pitot heads by atmospheriicing. Contrary to standard operational procedures, the flight crew had not activated the pitot head heaters.";
        schema:location          "Thiells" .

:1955-12-01-douglas-dc-3-id-630
        rdf:type                 schema:Event;
        ex:aircraftType          "Douglas DC-3";
        ex:groundFatalities      "0"^^xsd:int;
        ex:hour                  "02:00";
        ex:operator              "Cruzeiro";
        ex:passengersAboard      "6"^^xsd:int;
        ex:passengersFatalities  "6"^^xsd:int;
        ex:region                "Brazil";
        dcterms:date             "1955-12-01"^^xsd:date;
        dcterms:description      "Shortly after taking off, the No.1 engine lost power. The crew shut off the hydraulipump which caused the landing gear to stop retracting halfway. The resulting drag, caused the aircraft to lose altitude. The left wing struck a tree and the plane crashed and burned. Failure of the front bearing of the propeller shaft leading to disconnection of the reduction gear and left propeller and causing a sudden failure of the left engine.";
        schema:location          "Belem" .

