:1978-09-25-boeing-b-727-214---cessna-172-id-934
        rdf:type                 schema:Event;
        ex:aircraftType          "Boeing B-727-214 / Cessna 172";
        ex:groundFatalities      "7"^^xsd:int;
        ex:hour                  "09:02";
        ex:operator              "PacifiSouthwest Airlines / Private";
        ex:passengersAboard      "137"^^xsd:int;
        ex:passengersFatalities  "137"^^xsd:int;
        ex:region                "California";
        ex:route                 "Los Angeles - San Diego";
        dcterms:date             "1978-09-25"^^xsd:date;
        dcterms:description      "Midair collision. The PSA was descending and about to land at Lindbergh Field. The Cessna was climbing while engaged in practice approaches. The 727 overtook and struck the Cessna from the rear. The primary cause was the PSA crew lost sight of the Cessna and did not make that fact known to the ATC.  The ATC failed to realize from the PSA transmissions that they lost sight of the Cessna. One hundred thirty-five killed on the PSA, two on the Cessna and seven on the ground. Crew fatigue may have played a part in the accident as there are unofficial reports that the crew attended an all night party and had only 2 hours sleep from the night before.";
        schema:location          "San Diego" .

:1998-09-09-de-havilland-dash-2-float-plane-id-226
        rdf:type                 schema:Event;
        ex:aircraftType          "de Havilland Dash-2 float plane";
        ex:groundFatalities      "0"^^xsd:int;
        ex:hour                  "10:45";
        ex:operator              "Rusts Flying Service Inc.";
        ex:passengersAboard      "5"^^xsd:int;
        ex:passengersFatalities  "5"^^xsd:int;
        ex:region                "Alaska";
        ex:route                 "Anchorage, AK - Hoholitna River, AK";
        dcterms:date             "1998-09-09"^^xsd:date;
        dcterms:description      "The plane crashed while attempting to cross a mountain pass, following two other company airplanes in conditions described as five to seven miles visibility, 700 feet ceilings, clouds hanging on the mountainsides, and misty rain. The pilot's failure to maintain adequate airspeed which resulted in an inadvertent stall. Unfamiliarity with the geographiarea, the low clouds, his becoming disoriented, and the blind canyon into which he flew. The audible stall warning circuit breaker was found in the pulled (disabled) position.";
        schema:location          "Port Alsworth" .

<http://example.org/crash/1949-01-02-douglas-dc-3-(c-47a-dl)-id-4922>
        rdf:type                 schema:Event;
        ex:aircraftType          "Douglas DC-3 (C-47A-DL)";
        ex:groundFatalities      "0"^^xsd:int;
        ex:hour                  "22:05";
        ex:operator              "Seattle Air Charter";
        ex:passengersAboard      "30"^^xsd:int;
        ex:passengersFatalities  "14"^^xsd:int;
        ex:region                "Washington";
        ex:route                 "Seattle - New Haven";
        dcterms:date             "1949-01-02"^^xsd:date;
        dcterms:description      "The aircraft took off in fog with a group of Yale University students returning to school following their Christmas vacation.  About midway down the runway the plane swerved to the left and became airborne.  The left wing dropped the wing tip dragged on the runway.  After about 750 feet, the tail wheel made contact with the runway.  The engines were cut and the plane skidded for 700 feet until it hit a hanger and burst into flames.  The accident was caused by the crew's attempt to take off in an airplane which had formations of ice and frost on the surfaces of the wings.  In addition, the plane was overloaded by 1,500 lbs.";
        schema:location          "Seattle" .

