:1956-06-30-douglas-dc-7---lockheed-s-constellation-id-387
        rdf:type                 schema:Event;
        ex:aircraftType          "Douglas DC-7 / Lockheed S Constellation";
        ex:groundFatalities      "0"^^xsd:int;
        ex:hour                  "10:32";
        ex:operator              "United Air Lines / Trans World Airlines";
        ex:passengersAboard      "128"^^xsd:int;
        ex:passengersFatalities  "128"^^xsd:int;
        ex:region                "Arizona";
        ex:route                 "Los Angeles - Chicago / Los Angeles - Kansas City";
        dcterms:date             "1956-06-30"^^xsd:date;
        dcterms:description      "The TWA aircraft flying from Los Angeles to Kansas City and the United Aircraft flying from Los Angeles to Chicago collided over the Grand Canyon. Both planes were traveling about 320 mph. Both aircraft were flying in uncontrolled airspace. All  fifty-eight on the DC-7 and seventy on the Constellation killed. The pilots did not see each other in time to avoid the collision. It is not possible to determine why the pilots did not see each other, but the evidence suggests that it resulted from any one or a combination of the following factors: 1) Intervening clouds reducing time for visual separation 2) Visual limitations due to cockpit visibility 3) Preoccupation with normal cockpit duties.";
        schema:location          "Grand Canyon" .

