:1953-05-02-de-havilland-dh106-comet-1-id-2297
        rdf:type                 schema:Event;
        ex:aircraftType          "de Havilland DH106 Comet 1";
        ex:groundFatalities      "0"^^xsd:int;
        ex:hour                  "16:35";
        ex:operator              "British Overseas Airways";
        ex:passengersAboard      "43"^^xsd:int;
        ex:passengersFatalities  "43"^^xsd:int;
        ex:region                "India";
        ex:route                 "Calcutta - Delhi";
        dcterms:date             "1953-05-02"^^xsd:date;
        dcterms:description      "The aircraft crashed 6 minutes after taking off from Dum Dum Airport. The accident occurred during a violent thunderstorm. Wreckage indicated that the aircraft broke up in the air. Failure of both elevator spars caused by a heavy down-load that may have been associated with a pull-up maneuver. Severe gusts encountered in the thundersquall and overcontrolling or loss of control by the pilots when flying through the thunderstorm. The aircraft was nicknamed 'York Victor.'";
        schema:location          "West Bengal" .

:2008-11-08-cessna-206-id-4490
        rdf:type                 schema:Event;
        ex:aircraftType          "Cessna 206";
        ex:groundFatalities      "0"^^xsd:int;
        ex:hour                  "11:00";
        ex:operator              "East African Air Charters";
        ex:passengersAboard      "5"^^xsd:int;
        ex:passengersFatalities  "4"^^xsd:int;
        ex:region                "Tanzania";
        ex:route                 "Kampi ya Kanzi Airstrip - Loitokitok";
        dcterms:date             "2008-11-08"^^xsd:date;
        dcterms:description      "After taking off, the aircraft climbed to 12,500 feet. While cruising at this altitude, the aircraft was caught in a violent gust that initially pushed the plane upwards and towards the mountain. As the pilot tried to steer the aircraft away from the mountain, he realized that the airspeed had decayed to 40 kts., indicating an imminent stall. He decided to dive in order to gain air speed. As he did so the VSI indicated a rate of sink of 2,000 feet per minute while the airspeed remained at 40 kts. Visibility was poor and the pilot did not see the ground till impact at 14,399 feet. Four Italian tourists were killed. Only the pilot survived. It is therefore very likely that the aircraft was caught in a mountain wave that caused the sudden altitude gain and the subsequent stall.";
        schema:location          "Between Kibo & Mawenzi Peaks of Mt. Kilimanjaroi" .

